WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:07.120
This episode was made possible by generous
supporters on Patreon

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:07.940
Hey Crazies.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.520
In my last video, I explained how mass and
energy were the same thing

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.200
and that got a bunch of you wondering:

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.480
What the heck is energy?

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:18.560
I’ve said a lot about energy on this channel,
but it’s spread over multiple videos.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.480
It’s time to put it all in one place.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.900
Hmm, where do we start this?

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:24.040
Definitions?

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:25.500
We have to be careful though.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.180
Physics tends to use a lot of ordinary words
in not so ordinary ways

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.880
and “Energy” is no exception.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:36.060
There are a lot of ways we use this word in
the English language.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.500
I feel the energy of the spirits.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.100
Definitely not what we’re talking about.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.940
Oh man, I am so out of energy.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.660
Good example, but not really a definition.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:48.520
I get energy for the power grid.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.560
Again, good example, but not a definition.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:56.780
A typical definition for physical energy is
the ability or capacity to do work,

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.560
but that just raises more questions.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:00:59.940
What the heck is work?!

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.680
You don’t got to go to work work work work.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:07.280
Ok, the point is we can’t understand energy
without understanding work.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.180
We need both together.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.800
But let’s make something clear.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.300
Energy and work are just properties.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.480
They’re numbers.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.240
Objects and particles are physical things
that exist.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:21.180
Objects and particles have properties, like
energy and work.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.320
Work work work work.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.020
Take a ball, for example.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.100
We can label where it is by assigning a property called “position.”

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.300
We can label its motion by assigning a property called “velocity.”

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.360
An object can have motion and that motion is real,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.320
but the velocity we assign to it is not.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.860
It’s just a useful number.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.700
Properties are useful because they measure
something we want to know.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:49.080
All of these properties here, measure all
of these things here.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.660
We need to figure out exactly what energy
and work are measuring.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:56.500
Work is a little easier to understand, so
let’s try to figure that one out first.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.520
Everybody’s working for the weekend!

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:05.080
A typical definition for work is that it’s
done on an object by a force,

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:09.040
as long as that force contributes in some
way to how the object moves.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:11.260
Let’s say you wanted to do work on this
book.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:15.320
All you’d have to do is push on it with
enough force to make it move.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:19.200
That force times the book’s displacement
is the work done on the book.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.780
We know a force measures the influence or interaction amount,

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.919
so it makes perfect sense that forces are involved in work.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.260
The key here is this displacement.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:33.120
In order for work to be done on the book,
it has to move from one place to another.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.360
If the object doesn’t go anywhere, then
no work is done on it.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.140
No matter how much it feels like work to you.

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:43.360
A working class hero is something to be!

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.100
Stuff has to happen for work to be done.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.440
In fact, that tells us exactly what it is.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:52.680
Work is the amount of stuff that has happened
or, if you’re making predictions,

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.800
the amount of stuff that will happen.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.160
So what’s that make energy?

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.380
Well, let’s look back at the definition.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.370
If energy is the ability or capacity to do
work

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:04.920
and work is the amount of stuff that has or
will happen,

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:09.040
then energy is the amount of stuff that could
potentially happen.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.859
There is an upper limit to how much stuff
could happen

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:14.240
and that upper limit is measured as energy.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.439
Thanks for liking and sharing this.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.180
Wait a minute! What stuff?!

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:20.760
Do you think the crazies are going to be satisfied
with you just leaving it like that?!

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:23.180
OK, I guess you’re right.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:26.020
OK, so there is a lot of stuff that could
happen:

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:33.859
Lifting, swimming, climbing, dancing, swinging,
waving, running, slithering,

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:35.940
doing other things,

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:41.160
boiling, electrifying, chemical reactions, nuclear explosions.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.319
Let’s just say there are a lot of options.

00:03:43.319 --> 00:03:46.129
Right, so how could all those things be the
same thing?!

00:03:46.129 --> 00:03:50.140
They’re all examples of energy being used
to do work.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:53.880
If you’re lifting a box, you’re turning chemical energy in your body

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.820
into gravitational nergy.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:59.140
That change does work on the box, moving it
upward.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:02.300
You didn’t use all the energy inside your
body to do that work.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:03.420
Just a little bit.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:07.420
All of the energy in your body is all the
stuff you could possibly do.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.900
The work is what you actually do.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:14.580
That box now has energy it can use to do stuff,
like fall down.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.200
The gravitational energy is gradually turned
into kinetic energy on the way down.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.620
That’s not even all the energy inside that box.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:26.220
Sure, it has gravitational and kinetic energy,
but it also has thermal energy because it’s

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:28.840
not an absolute zero and a whole list of other energies

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.220
tied up in the material.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.340
None of which are getting used to do work.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.040
The only work done is the box falling.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.960
That’s the stuff that’s happening.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:38.640
So how do we measure these things, anyway?

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.460
Well, we don’t, at least not directly.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:47.000
Unlike position or velocity, energy and work
are properties that are hidden from view.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.240
We have to calculate their value by measuring
something else.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:54.600
If it’s gravitational energy, we measure
a distance from some reference location,

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:55.840
usually the ground,

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.520
and calculate the energy using some equation.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:03.280
Twice as high means twice the energy, meaning
it can do twice as much stuff.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.940
If it’s kinetic energy, we calculate it with a measured speed.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:11.620
Twice the speed means four times the energy,
or four times as much stuff.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.540
There are equations for finding all types
of energy

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:18.640
and every single one adds to what an object could possibly do.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:21.660
If you can change energy from one type to another

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:25.080
or move it from one object to another, you’re doing work.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:26.780
Something will be happening.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:29.280
Working in a coalmine, going down down.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.920
There are a lot of different places we can
find energy,

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.160
so it helps to split them into categories.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.500
We could take the list and organize it based
on where we find them,

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.180
but there’s something we can do that’s
a bit more useful.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:44.300
All energies can be described by two generic
types:

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:49.300
Potential Energy, related to location, and
Kinetic Energy, related to motion.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:50.800
That’s all there really is.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:54.170
Remember, the purpose of energy is to show
us what could happen

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:57.900
and the only way things happen is if they
move from one place to another.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:02.600
Location and motion pretty much cover that,
so can you label all of these

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:05.240
as potential and/or kinetic energy.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:09.020
Nuclear energy is just the potential energy
of protons and neutrons.

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:12.760
Chemical energy is just the potential energy
in chemical bonds.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:17.840
Thermal energy is wiggly kinetic energy plus
some material potential energy.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.680
But don’t location and motion depend on
your point of view?

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.520
Yep, and that’s more evidence that energy
isn’t something tangible.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:27.060
Energy is not a thing by itself.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.700
It’s a property of things,
so there’s no reason in particular we’d

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:32.680
all have to agree on how much is there.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:35.800
If location and motion are relative to the
observer,

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:38.120
then so are potential and kinetic energy.

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:41.760
There’s always some kind of reference point where you define zero.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:44.920
For kinetic energy, it’s usually your own speed.

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:50.140
Two observers moving at different speeds aren’t going to agree on the motion of a third object,

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:52.860
so they’re not going to agree on its energy either.

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:56.010
That’s perfectly fine because energy isn’t a tangible thing.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:57.120
It’s just a number.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.820
A similar thing happens for potential energy.

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:03.080
For gravitational energy, the reference is
usually the ground,

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:05.640
but there’s nothing special about that choice.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.560
You could move that reference around and it’s totally fine.

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:13.360
What matters is the difference in energy between two events across time.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:14.700
That’s the work done.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.260
That’s what’s actually happening.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:18.820
She works hard for the money!

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:20.180
So what the heck is energy?!

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:22.840
Energy is amount of stuff that could happen.

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:26.480
But, in the end, all that really matters is
what does happen

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:28.040
and that takes work.

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:29.840
So, got any questions about energy?

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.240
Please ask in the comments.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:33.020
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:35.320
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like to keep up with us.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:39.240
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:42.880
The featured comment comes from Dale Hildebrand who said:

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:46.260
The idea of relativistic mass can lead to false conclusions.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:47.760
Well, yeah, sure it can.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:50.700
I even said in the video that it can get really confusing.

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:53.460
But that doesn’t mean relativistic mass isn’t real.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:55.820
It just means that it stopped being useful.

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:57.580
Anyway, thanks for watching!

